interactive_layout:
# The Modal Logic of Forcing — An Interactive Tour

Mathematics has a hidden freedom. Some questions about infinity — most famously the **Continuum Hypothesis** — can be neither proved nor disproved from the standard axioms. There are consistent universes where they hold and consistent universes where they fail, and the machine that travels between these universes is called *forcing*. This tour turns that picture into a logic you can play with.

## 1. Necessity and possibility, set-theoretically

Read $\Box p$ as "*$p$ holds in every forcing extension*" and $\Diamond p$ as "*$p$ holds in some forcing extension*". These are dual: $\Diamond p \leftrightarrow \lnot\Box\lnot p$. Start by evaluating sentences across the eight-world multiverse and watch necessity and possibility come alive.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## 2. The rules forcing obeys

Forcing is reflexive (you can force trivially over yourself), transitive (iterated forcing is forcing), and directed (any two extensions have a common further extension). Each property hands us one modal axiom, and together they give exactly the system **S4.2**. The algorithm below certifies a frame, and the chart pairs every axiom with the condition that yields it.

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:2}}

The evaluation engine underneath everything is a simple structural recursion:

{{algorithm:0}}

## 3. The law that fails — forcing has no undo button

The stronger system **S5** would require forcing to be reversible: whatever is true stays recoverable, $p \to \Box\Diamond p$. But forcing is a one-way street. Step through the sink frame and see precisely where the argument breaks.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:0}}

## 4. Independence is contingency

A statement is *independent* when it is true in some universe and false in another — which is exactly modal *contingency* $\Diamond p \wedge \Diamond\lnot p$. Toggle the worlds and confirm the two notions always agree.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

## 5. The Continuum Hypothesis, modally

Gödel's constructible universe satisfies CH; a Cohen extension refutes it. So both $\Diamond\,\mathrm{CH}$ and $\Diamond\,\lnot\mathrm{CH}$ hold: CH is contingent, and therefore *not necessary* — a one-line modal restatement of Cohen's theorem. Explore the whole multiverse as a cube, and run the numerical checks.

{{visualization:1}}

{{demo:0}}

{{demo:1}}

## Takeaway

The multiverse is not a metaphor but a structured modal object. Its laws of possibility and necessity are exactly those of S4.2 — validated by reflexivity, transitivity, and directedness, and stopping short of S5 because forcing cannot be undone.

